{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And Sarai said, etc. My wrong is, etc.</b> One needs to know why Sarah submitted her complaint against Abraham, and added specification, saying ״חֲמָסִי עָלֶיךָ״ — which appears to indicate that she intended to diminish his honor. And if she intended this regarding Hagar, her words are not correct according to what appears, for it was Hagar who slighted the honor of her mistress, and her complaint should be against her, not against Abraham. Further, one needs to know the intent of Abraham's words in his reply, <b>Behold, your maidservant</b> etc., which appears as though he accepted Sarai's complaint, only that he is setting matters right from here forward; and if he had done no wrong, should he not have said ״מַה תָּרִיב אֵלַי״ (Why do you contend with me)?" "<b>Now</b> the matter will be explained by prefacing the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 45:1), and this is their language: ״וְלָהּ שִׁפְחָה מִצְרִית״ — she was a melog maidservant, and he was liable for her maintenance and was not permitted to sell her. Reish Lakish raises the question: what is taught by the term melog? He said to them: as you say melog, melog — thus far. We see then that Hagar's body itself was owned by Sarah and her fruits belonged to Abraham for melog. And regarding the matter of the children of a melog maidservant if she gave birth, they disagreed in tractate Ketubot page 79 — Chananya and the Sages. The Sages say: the child of melog belongs to the wife, the reason being that we are concerned for death and the principal is consumed. And Chananya holds it belongs to the husband, for we are not concerned for death. Further, we say in Gittin page 39, Rav Ashi said Rav said: ״A slave who married a free woman in the presence of his master — his master has made him a free man,״ and the Talmud explains it where his master married him off to a free woman — thus far. And they explain there that from the implication he freed him, for otherwise he would not have facilitated a transgression through him to marry him to a free woman — thus far. And the same applies to a maidservant whose master married her to a free man — he has made her a free woman. And our master Rabbi Yosef Karo wrote in Yoreh De'ah, Laws of Slaves, section 267, and this is his language: one who lies with his maidservant has made her a free woman, and specifically for the sake of marriage, but not for the sake of marriage he has not made her a free woman — thus far. Now, regarding one who lies with her without specification, the Rif and the Rambam disagree: according to the Rif (Yevamot chapter 2) from the implication as well he has made her a free woman, and according to the Rambam (Laws of Slaves chapter 9) he has not made her a free woman unless he explicitly specified for the sake of marriage." "<b>And with this</b> we will come to explain the matter, for when Sarah saw that Hagar was disparaging her honor she was angered and shaken: how could it be that a maidservant would slight her mistress? And by implication Abraham sensed the matter from the fact that Sarah was storming against him before informing him of the matter. And even though she told him afterward, this was not the manner of informing but words of indignation, and the knowledge — she already recognized that he knew. And she judged in this two divisions of law: the first, that Abraham explicitly revealed that he lay with her for the sake of marriage — or even if he lay with her without specification, Hagar considers herself a free woman according to the Rif. And the second, that Hagar thought in her mind that she had gone forth to freedom, based on the law they stated in Gittin that we cited above: a slave who married a free woman in the presence of his master goes forth to freedom, and likewise the reverse — a maidservant who was married to a free man, her mistress has made her a free woman, and thereby the bondage of Hagar was dissolved. Therefore she cried out before Abraham, for upon him lay her complaint and not upon Hagar, for Hagar according to the law as she thinks in her mind cannot be blamed; but ״my wrong is upon you״ — for you concur with her understanding which is not according to the law." "And she stated claims to refute and uproot the sides of the laws from which Hagar's error regarding freedom had emerged. And that is her saying <b>I gave my maidservant into your bosom</b> — meaning, this maidservant is mine, and you have in her nothing but melog of fruits. And even if you should lie with her explicitly for the sake of marriage, she has no law of emancipation through this, for she is not your maidservant for you to emancipate her, even if you say so explicitly. And if from the angle of the claim of one who marries off his maidservant to a free man, etc. — that applies specifically if he gave her without specification, as they gave the reason that from the implication he does not transgress the negative prohibition of ״לֹא תִהְיֶה קְדֵשָׁה וְלֹא יִהְיֶה קָדֵשׁ״ which is a warning to a free man, etc. — but I did not leave the matter unspecified; rather I explained the matter to you explicitly, that I did not emancipate her — she is still my maidservant even though she is in your bosom. And likewise you will find in her first words to him, when she said ״בֹּא נָא אֶל שִׁפְחָתִי״ — meaning, even after he lies with her she is still her maidservant; for she should have said ״בֹּא נָא אֶל הָגָר״." "<b>Furthermore</b> I found a midrash of our Sages of blessed memory in Bereshot (Genesis Rabbah 45:3), and this is their language: ״וַתִּתֵּן אוֹתָהּ לְאַבְרָם אִישָׁהּ״ — and not to another — thus far. Here our Sages of blessed memory examined the word ״אִישָׁהּ״ which should not have been necessary to say, and they concluded that it comes to restrict. And one needs to know: for what purpose was this restriction required? And according to our words, the matter needs to be said to indicate that she is still her maidservant, and she gave her only to Abraham. And if Abraham wished to give her to another, or to send her on her own to do as she pleased, he has no authority — from this you learn that she is still her maidservant." "And Abraham acknowledged the justice of her words, that the law was with her in her claim, and there are two ways to understand this. The first: it is possible that Abraham's intent was the same from beginning to end, and even so he came upon her for a reason that I will explain. Or, he did not think so at first, and his understanding was that she was intended as a wife and was a freedwoman, for he did not think that Sarah had intended what she now revealed her mind to be; and had he thought so, it is possible that he would not have come upon her, by law (Deuteronomy 23:18): ״לֹא יִהְיֶה קָדֵשׁ״. And so you will find that during the span of fourteen years from the birth of Ishmael until Isaac was born, he did not again cohabit with her." "<b>And the reason</b> that Sarah was not stringent about giving a <b>maidservant</b> to a free man, and likewise Abraham — as regards what is said that he understood Sarah's words and did not concern himself — know that according to the plain sense we may say that they had not yet been commanded concerning this. And although they were meticulous to fulfill even the rabbinic enactments, on account of prophecy they would transgress commandments for the time being. And Rambam already wrote in the Book of Knowledge, chapter 9, in the Laws of the Foundations of the Torah, that if a prophet comes with a statement from Him, may He be blessed, that Israel should transgress — even many — one transgression for the time being, it is permitted to do so; like Elijah on Mount Carmel (I Kings 18), and likewise Abraham acted according to Sarah's word, for she was a greater prophetess than Abraham, as they said of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 1) on the verse: ״כֹּל אֲשֶׁר תֹּאמַר אֵלֶיךָ שָׂרָה שְׁמַע בְּקוֹלָהּ״. Therefore when she said to him ״בֹּא נָא אֶל שִׁפְחָתִי״ he agreed to the words of the prophetess. And there was a need for the matter to be so, for through Hagar's being her maidservant, the repugnant portion that had been attached to Abraham from days of old was drawn out in her, and that impurity emerged in Ishmael, and a complete and pure light remained in Isaac. Had Hagar been freed, not all of the impurity would have emerged, apart from the good portion emerging with it, and a great breach would have resulted from the matter — and YHWH, may He be blessed, works His wonders for us. Or perhaps this is what Sarah intended when she said יִשְׁפֹּט הַשֹּׁפֵט בֵּינִי וּבֵינֶךָ, for she feared that Abraham would say: and how do you consent to him being a kadesh? Therefore she said: He, the Judge, shall judge between him and her, etc." "And immediately he acknowledged the justice of her words, upon him be peace. And when Sarah knew that there was no refutation of her claims and Hagar was still her maidservant — <b>וַתְּעַנֶּהָ שָׂרַי</b> ... <b>וַתִּבְרַח</b> etc. — meaning that Hagar did not accept the verdict, and thought that they had wronged her, and she had the status of a free woman, since her mistress had given her to a free man — the great man Abraham our father, upon him be peace — she surely went out to freedom, and she had not heard the condition that Sarah had stipulated; therefore she fled. And the sons of Elohim encountered her and informed her that she was Sarah's maidservant, and she accepted the verdict: <b>וַתֹּאמֶר מִפְּנֵי שָׂרַי גְּבִרְתִּי</b> etc." "<b>And there is</b> room to examine regarding Ishmael whether we have any acquisition in him or not. It is taught in Torat Kohanim, parashat Behar Sinai, and this is its language: From where do you say that an Israelite who cohabits with his maidservant and she bears from him a son — are you permitted to enslave him as a servant? Scripture says: ״אֲשֶׁר יִהְיוּ לְךָ מֵאֵת הַגּוֹיִם אֲשֶׁר סְבִיבוֹתֵיכֶם״ — and not from the Canaanites who are in the land; end of quotation. Meaning: there was no need to say ״אֲשֶׁר יִהְיוּ לְךָ״, and the implication of the word ״יִהְיוּ לְךָ״ is forced, since he should have said ״אֲשֶׁר תִּקְנֶה״. From this they expound ״אֲשֶׁר יִהְיוּ לְךָ״ — meaning: you will beget from the nations, that is, an Israelite who cohabits with his maidservant. And their saying וְלֹא מִן הַכְּנַעֲנִי etc. — meaning, for this one has the law (Deuteronomy 20:16): ״לֹא תְחַיֶּה כָּל נְשָׁמָה״. From this it follows that Ishmael and his seed are acquired by us as a complete acquisition — not only according to the opinion of the Rabbis that a child of a melog maidservant belongs to the woman, and we are heirs of Sarah; but even according to the opinion of Hanania — behold, Abraham gave all that he had to Isaac, and Ishmael is included, and he belongs to us among the peoples that YHWH has covenanted to give us, and from their inclusivity is Edom — he and his portion within Ishmael." "<b>And the faithful testimony to our words</b> is what the Creator, may He be exalted, said (Genesis 21:13): ״וְגַם אֶת בֶּן הָאָמָה״ — behold, He called her the mother and called him her son and not His son. For you will find regarding the sons of the maidservants Bilhah and Zilpah that Scripture was precise to say of them (Genesis 30:5): ״וַתֵּלֶד לְיַעֲקֹב בֵּן״, and likewise it said of the second: ״בֵּן שֵׁנִי לְיַעֲקֹב״, and likewise of the third it said: ״וַתֵּלֶד זִלְפָּה שִׁפְחַת לֵאָה לְיַעֲקֹב בֵּן״, and likewise of the fourth it said: ״בֵּן שֵׁנִי לְיַעֲקֹב״. Behold, in all of them He was precise to designate them by the name sons of Jacob, for they were freed; but here YHWH said the reverse — ״בֶּן הָאָמָה״ — and said further to him: ״כִּי בְיִצְחָק יִקָּרֵא לְךָ זָרַע״ (Genesis 21:12)." "<b>And this</b> is not included among the ten nations that HaShem said He would give them to you, for this one we merited by our own effort and there is no need for a giving in it."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {1 "The abbreviation הרי״ף is resolved as Rabbi Yitzchak Alfasi (the Rif); the abbreviation רס״ז is resolved as ר\"ס ז which equals 267."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:26.209639793Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}